  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 405 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nStacked low pressure system will move across southern Canada\ntoday bringing strong winds and mainly dry conditions to the\nstate. Strong westerly winds are still expected to develop later\nthis morning and through this afternoon. The latest forecast\nkeeps the strongest winds and wind gusts generally along and\nnorth of the Highway 2 corridor. However, sustained winds in\nexcess of 30 mph are now forecast to be a tier of counties \nsouth of these strongest winds. Thus expanded the Wind Advisory \nslightly south to account for this. Counties along the Canadian \nborder could see sustained winds approach High Wind Criteria \ntoday in excess of 40 mph and wind gusts near 60 MPH if the \nstrong 850 mb wind can mix down. HREF max wind ensembles keep \nthe strongest wind gusts in Canada currently, although forecast \nsoundings showing steep low level lapse rates this afternoon \ncould attempt to mix these stronger winds. For now kept with a \nWind Advisory given the low confidence in these stronger winds. \nThere still remains a threat for near critical to perhaps a \nbrief period of critical fire weather conditions today. Areas of\nbiggest concern remain counties just to the north of the \nMissouri River along with areas south and west of the Missouri \nRiver. Red Flag hours are 1 to 3 hours for most of these areas \nthis afternoon. The uncertainty comes with fuel status, given \nmost of these areas received a wetting rain this weekend. Most \nareas will have somewhat steep low level lapse rates today, \nalthough the stronger portion of the 850 mph jet is in the north\ntoday where cooler temperatures will keep higher RH this \nafternoon. Given these uncertainties will hold off on Fire \nWeather highlights for now. Increased red flag hours or further \nnudge southward of the 850 mb jet could nudge towards issuance \nof a fire weather product and will have to be monitored closely \ntoday. Otherwise, look for cooler temperatures in the 70s to \nlower 80s today along with mainly clear and dry conditions. \nWinds will diminish tonight, while dry conditions remain. Lows \ntonight will be cool for some areas and in the mid 40s to mid \n50s. Flat ridging aloft will push the mentioned low out of the \narea on Tuesday. Temperatures look to remain cooler with highs \nin the 70s to lower 80s. Later in the day through Tuesday night \na weak upper level wave with monsoonal moisture looks to move \nacross the northern plains. How far north this wave gets will \ndetermine if any precipitation reaches North Dakota. Today keeps\nrain chances more in SD, although some indications areas along \nand south of Interstate 94 could have at least a slight chance \nof rain later Tuesday through Tuesday night. Lows Tuesday night \nwill be seasonable and near 50 degrees. Increased RH Tuesday \nnight could lead to some patchy fog, depending on cloud cover \nand winds.\n\nClusters fairly consistent on a strong ridge developing across\nthe central US while an upper level low remains across the\nPacific NW mid to late week. The result for ND will be a mix of\nupper level ridging and southwest flow. Days with stronger\nsouthwest flow could see chances for showers and thunderstorms\nreturn as monsoonal moisture and upper level waves moves\nthrough. Right now the highest chances for this look to come \nWednesday night through Thursday and perhaps again this weekend.\nThere is some limited instability and shear with this mid week \nwave, perhaps returning some isolated strong to severe storms. \nTemperatures could go on a warming trend at least mid to late \nwork week. Forecast highs in the 90s may also return by late \nweek. This warming trend could bring lower RH values in the \nafternoon, possibly bringing some increased fire weather \nconcerns. Temperatures this weekend may stay above normal, \nalthough NBM spreads are larger. Some clusters see a slight \nbreakdown to the ridge bringing cooler temperatures, while most \nkeep it in place. Of note for this weekend is the potential for\nmonsoonal like moisture to stream into the area with the \ncluster solution that breaks the ridge down. This could lead to \ncooler temperatures and perhaps some increased chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms. This more active and cooler solution \nis currently considered an outlier, and the outgoing forecast \nfor now maintains only slight chances for precipitation with \nnear normal temperatures.",
